/-!
# Validity of the exp-580 decision rules, and why the control arm fired spuriously

Experiment 580 rested on three inferential devices.  This file proves the two
that are sound and proves the exact failure mode of the third.

1. **Permutation (randomization) p-values.**  `permPValue_valid`: for the
   randomization distribution generated by a finite group of relabellings, the
   p-value is super-uniform — the test rejects at level `α` with probability at
   most `α`, in finite samples and with no distributional assumption.  This
   licences the reported permutation p-values (family B: p = 0.34).

2. **Bonferroni multiplicity control.**  `bonferroni`: if each of `m`
   p-values is super-uniform, then the probability that *any* of them falls
   below `α / m` is at most `α`.  This is exactly the correction that killed the
   pooled rich-vs-poor KS result (raw p = 0.0038 across the tested family).

3. **The occupancy logistic regression (design B).**  The ledger flagged that
   design B fired on dense size-matched controls with quasi-separation and odds
   ratios clipped at `e^{±30}`.  `logistic_no_mle` proves this is not a
   numerical accident but a theorem: if some direction `w` separates the
   labelled scores, the log-likelihood has **no maximiser at all** — it is
   strictly increasing along the ray `t ↦ t·w`, stays strictly negative, and has
   supremum `0`.  Every fitted coefficient is then an artefact of the optimiser's
   stopping rule, so a "significant" odds ratio from such a fit carries no
   inferential content.  Design B is therefore unreliable exactly where the
   ledger flag says it is.
-/

